WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:12.948
My name's Abdullah Kabani.

00:00:12.948 --> 00:00:15.310
I own a phone-repair company.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:16.376
I'm going to walk you through

00:00:16.376 --> 00:00:18.334
how to restore a cracked iPad.

00:00:18.334 --> 00:00:20.960
This is a fifth-generation Apple iPad.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.320
The person that brought this
in, if I remember correctly,

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.320
one day he just looked at
it and he was really angry

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.090
and he just threw it
against the garage wall,

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.900
and that's how this happened.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.350
Since this frame is bent like a banana,

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.320
it crushed just about
everything in its way,

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.650
including the screen,
the battery, and the LCD.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:37.666
To replace the frame,

00:00:37.666 --> 00:00:41.016
we practically have to
rebuild the entire iPad,

00:00:41.016 --> 00:00:42.960
reusing all the parts that we can

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.727
and replacing the ones that we need to.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.320
This screen is destroyed,

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.300
so we're just going to yank it out.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.510
To get started, we have
to loosen up the adhesive

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:58.030
that's holding the screen
down into the frame.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.910
Normally we use some heat with a heat pad,

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:02.385
but this frame was so bent

00:01:02.385 --> 00:01:03.868
that it wouldn't sit down flat,

00:01:03.868 --> 00:01:05.269
so we have to use a heat gun.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:10.520
As I heat up and loosen up the adhesive,

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.762
I'll use my blade to pry
the glass up from the frame.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.458
I'll carefully scrape out

00:01:35.458 --> 00:01:37.383
the remaining glass in the frame,

00:01:37.383 --> 00:01:38.908
because that'll get in the way

00:01:38.908 --> 00:01:41.246
when I try to remove this LCD.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.260
I'll remove the remaining
glass with a scalpel.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:55.120
As soon as I lift up the LCD

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.890
and expose the battery connection,

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:58.610
I'll use this little battery separator

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.300
to remove any power supply

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.500
and the risk of shorting something out.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.820
Frame replacements require
a whole lot of work,

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:08.861
because I have to remove

00:02:08.861 --> 00:02:11.302
every single component in this iPad

00:02:11.302 --> 00:02:12.992
and put it into the new frame.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.620
Every component is important
and serves a purpose,

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.070
so I have to be really careful
not to damage anything,

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:25.160
'cause we're going to reuse it.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.020
The most important part
is the motherboard.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.130
My customer got really
lucky that the motherboard

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.653
didn't get damaged with
the bend in the frame.

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:35.730
In my experience, all lithium batteries

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.070
have about a two-and-a-half-
to three-year life span

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:39.620
before they start acting up,

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.220
and this iPad's been sitting
in a really cold garage

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.760
for a long time, so since
we're doing all this work,

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.550
we figured we might as
well replace the battery

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:49.280
so that it'll last a lot longer.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.460
I don't like using too
much heat on the battery,

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:52.910
so I'll use a little bit of acetone

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:54.470
to loosen up the adhesive,

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.879
then jump straight to my
spudger and pry that thing out.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.200
I don't actually have to remove
the battery for this repair,

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.483
but it'll make getting
everything else out a lot easier.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.730
In order to safely do this repair,

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:13.710
I have to be meticulous
with my hand movements,

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:18.150
because any slip can damage
a cable or a battery,

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.283
start a fire.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.900
To get started with the reassembly,

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.420
we'll install the power and volume buttons

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.895
just before installing the motherboard.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:37.220
I'm using my screwdriver and some screws

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:38.670
to hold it in place.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:42.963
Some components require screws,

00:03:42.963 --> 00:03:45.054
and others just adhesive.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:53.440
We use a higher-than-original-quality
aftermarket battery

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.400
that uses Texas Instruments capacitors

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.580
that regulate the power voltage.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:01.270
I use my tweezers, my screwdriver,

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:02.960
and my battery-separating tool,

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.080
because even though I'm
putting the battery back in,

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:08.220
I still don't want it to give
any power to the motherboard

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.373
until I have everything else plugged in.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.640
I'll use some heat to
loosen up the adhesive

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.310
underneath the home button.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:19.570
We'll have to transfer that
one over to the new screen,

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:22.870
otherwise the fingerprint
functionality won't work anymore.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:28.425
Broken glass shards often get stuck

00:04:28.425 --> 00:04:30.325
to the adhesive underneath
the home button,

00:04:30.325 --> 00:04:32.141
so I have to be really careful,

00:04:32.141 --> 00:04:34.256
because those can damage the new screen.

00:04:41.077 --> 00:04:42.368
To readhere the home button,

00:04:42.368 --> 00:04:43.393
we'll use a mixture of

00:04:43.393 --> 00:04:46.207
slow- and fast-setting industrial glue.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.713
I use four different types of glue

00:04:50.713 --> 00:04:53.110
to seal the screen down onto the frame.

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:54.460
I'll start by prepping the frame

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.960
with some adhesion promoter.

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:00.522
I'll also use some
industrial-strength glue.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:09.280
And a few drops of superglue
so that it sets fast

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:10.847
just before we clamp it.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.000
When I'm reassembling an iPad,

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.663
even the tiniest speck of dust
can ruin the entire repair.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.160
A fingerprint could get in
between the layers of the screen

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:27.880
and drive someone insane,

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.050
and if it's already sealed up,

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:30.200
then we'd have to open it up

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.016
and do about an hour of cleaning,

00:05:32.016 --> 00:05:33.706
so we have to be very careful.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.180
I use the clamps to
secure everything together

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.072
so I can restore the
original airtight seal.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:50.450
Normally I would have to
leave them on for days,

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:53.730
but thankfully, since I used
a few drops of superglue,

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:56.063
I only have to leave it in for 20 minutes.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.100
This specific repair took a lot more work

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:08.000
than your average iPad repair,

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:08.860
and I'm just grateful

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:11.210
that we were able to
bring it back to life.

